course: biology
lesson: 287
title: Farm animals: milk, eggs, wool and meat
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Your breakfast is a farm tour: milk from a cow, an egg from a hen, maybe bacon from a pig, and the wool jumper you pull on came from a sheep.
explain: A cow gives milk because, like every mammal mother, she makes it for her calf. A dairy cow can give 25 to 30 litres a day, and today many are milked by machines or even robots that the cow walks into by herself.
explain: Animals are not machines, though. A cow chews grass twice, a pig rolls in mud to keep cool, and a sheepdog moves sheep the way a wolf would, because sheep bunch together for safety.
explain: Cartoons get a lot wrong: a bull does not hate the colour red, and a goat does not munch tin cans. Real farm animals are cleverer and stranger than the cartoon ones.
fact: Cows give milk after they have had a calf, because mammals make milk for their babies; a dairy cow gives about 25 to 30 litres a day.
fact: Milk is cooled, heated to kill germs and then bottled.
fact: A hen lays about one egg a day in good times, with or without a cockerel; shop eggs are not fertilised and could never hatch.
fact: The colour of an eggshell depends on the hen's breed, not on how healthy the egg is.
fact: A cow has one stomach with four parts; microbes in the first part break down grass, and the cow brings it back up to chew again, which is called chewing the cud.
fact: Microbes in a cow's stomach make methane, a gas that warms the planet; most of it leaves in burps, not farts, and farmers are testing seaweed in feed to cut it.
fact: Pigs sweat very little, so they roll in mud to cool down and protect their skin from the sun; they are clean when given space and among the cleverest farm animals.
fact: Sheep flock together for safety, a sheepdog moves them like a wolf would, the shepherd directs the dog with whistles, and sheep are sheared about once a year, so their fleece can be spun into wool and they stay cool in summer.
fact: Ham, bacon and sausages come from pigs; some families eat no pork for religious reasons, Jews and Muslims for example, and some eat no meat at all.
fact: Free-range hens can go outside, caged hens live inside buildings, and many countries have banned the smallest cages.
fact: In Morocco goats climb argan trees to eat the fruit; goats test things with their mouths but cannot really eat tin cans.
fact: Bulls cannot see red well; it is the moving cape that makes a bull charge, and a waved blue cape would work just the same.
fact: About half of the fish people eat now comes from fish farms, such as salmon, carp and prawn farms, which can take pressure off wild fish but can also spread disease and pollution.
fact: There are about a billion cattle on Earth.
exercise: Why does a cow make milk? | Like all mammal mothers, she makes it for her calf | Grass turns into milk inside every cow, even the bulls | Farmers pour water into her | Only bulls make milk
exercise: About how much milk can a dairy cow give in a day? | About 25 to 30 litres | About one cup | About 1,000 litres a day | About half a litre
exercise: Why is milk heated before it is bottled? | To kill germs | To make it white | To make it sweeter | To turn it into cheese
exercise: Can an egg from the shop hatch into a chick? | No, shop eggs are not fertilised | Yes, if you keep it warm a week | Yes, but only brown ones | No, because they are boiled first
exercise: What makes an eggshell brown or white? | The breed of the hen | How healthy the egg is | How old the hen is | What colour the farmer likes
exercise: How many stomachs does a cow really have? | One stomach with four parts | Four separate stomachs | Two stomachs | No stomach, only a gut
exercise: What is chewing the cud? | Bringing grass back up to chew it again | Chewing on a piece of wood from the fence | Eating grass very fast | Licking salt off a stone
exercise: How does most of a cow's methane leave its body? | In burps | In farts | In its milk | Through its skin
exercise: Why do pigs roll in mud? | They hardly sweat, so mud cools them and blocks the sun | They are dirty animals that like strong smells more than anything | To hide from the farmer | To clean their teeth
exercise: How does a shepherd tell the sheepdog where to go? | With whistles | By ringing a bell on the sheep | By writing on a board | The dog decides alone
exercise: Which animal do ham, bacon and sausages come from? | The pig | The cow | The sheep | The goat
exercise: What is the difference with free-range hens? | They can go outside, unlike caged hens | They lay no eggs at all | They are wild birds from the woods | They live inside in the farmer's own house
exercise: Why do goats climb argan trees in Morocco? | To eat the fruit | To escape from wolves | To build nests | To look for water
exercise: What really makes a bull charge at the cape? | The moving cape | The red colour | The smell of the cloth | The music in the arena
exercise: About how much of the fish people eat comes from fish farms? | About half | Almost none | All of it | About one in a hundred
exercise: Why does sheep's wool get sheared? | So it can be spun into wool, and the sheep stays cool | Because the wool turns poisonous if it is left on the sheep | Because the sheep eats it | To stop the sheep flying away
test: In a Bugs Bunny cartoon a bull sees a red cape and goes wild with rage at the colour. What is real? | Bulls cannot see red well; the waving cape makes them charge | Bulls hate red more than any other colour, so it makes them furious | Bulls charge only at green | Bulls never charge at anything
test: «a cow's got four stomachs, everybody knows that» What is closer to the truth? | One stomach with four parts | Yes, four separate stomachs | Two stomachs and a spare | One stomach with one part
test: «brown eggs are healthier innit, that's why they cost more» What is right? | No, the colour depends only on the hen's breed | Yes, brown eggs have twice the goodness | Yes, white eggs are bleached and unhealthy | No, white eggs are always healthier
test: Count them: 5 cows and 3 hens in the yard. How many legs? | 26 | 32 | 16 | 24
test: Count them: a goat tree in Morocco has 7 goats in it. How many horns, if each goat has 2? | 14 | 7 | 21 | 28
test: «pigs are filthy, look at them in that mud» What is right? | They roll in mud to cool down, and are clean when they have space | Yes, pigs love dirt more than food, and roll in it to smell worse | Pigs are the dirtiest of all animals | Mud is their food
test: What would happen if a sheepdog ran straight at the flock from every side at once? | The sheep would scatter in panic instead of moving together | The sheep would sit down calmly | The sheep would follow the dog home | Nothing, sheep ignore dogs
test: «what's chewing cud, gross, why'd she spit up her lunch» Why do cows do it? | Chewing grass twice helps break it down so microbes can digest it | She is ill, and is being sick because the grass was bad for her | She does not like grass | She is feeding her calf
test: In a cartoon a goat chews a tin can and swallows it. What is real? | Goats test things with their mouths but cannot eat tin cans | Goats live on tin cans | Goats only eat metal | Goats never put anything in their mouths, not even grass or leaves
test: «mate won't have my bacon sandwich, he's Muslim, weird» What is good manners? | Respecting that some families do not eat pork | Telling him to try it anyway | Hiding pork in his food | Laughing at his lunch
test: Why might a farmer add a little seaweed to cattle food? | It can cut the methane cows burp out | It makes the milk green | It stops cows from eating | It makes cows swim
test: What would happen to a cow that never had a calf? | She would not make milk | She would make twice as much milk | She would lay eggs | Her milk would be chocolate
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a bull charges at the cape in the arena. | The movement of the cape, the animal being unable to perceive red distinctly | An innate aversion to the colour red, which provokes the animal to fury | The sound of the trumpets | The scent of the matador
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the digestive system of the cow. | It possesses a single stomach of four compartments and re-chews its food | It possesses four entirely separate stomachs | It digests grass without the assistance of microbes | It possesses no stomach whatsoever
exam: Ascertain the factor determining the colour of a hen's eggshell. | The breed of the hen | The nutritional value of the egg | The presence of a cockerel | The season of laying
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the wallowing of pigs in mud? | It is a means of cooling, pigs being largely unable to sweat | It is evidence of an innate preference for filth | It is a method of obtaining food | It is a form of courtship
